 ## Overview
-EWA Splatting is a high-quality volume rendering technique that eliminates aliasing artifacts by replacing the standard footprint function with a band-limited resampling filter [S1, unofficial][S2][S8, unofficial]. It solves the problem of visual artifacts caused by incorrect visibility determination and sampling rate changes in perspective projections, which occur when reconstruction kernels overlap or when the sampling rate falls below the Nyquist frequency [S1, S8, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from basic splatting by combining an elliptical Gaussian reconstruction kernel with a Gaussian low-pass filter to avoid aliasing, and from other variants by using a rigorous affine approximation of the projective mapping to derive an analytic 2D footprint [S2][S8, unofficial].
